hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in book one of  his work on free choice of the will in  the dialog with his student Augustine  sets out what he calls the due or proper  ordering of a human being and what he  means there is is considering the human  being as you might say a complex of  different powers or faculties how is it  that these ought to be arranged in  relation to each other or if you want to  look at it another way which of them  should be ruling or determining what a  person is doing through the agency of  the will now Augustine thinks that there  is something that he calls the eternal  law and part of this includes a certain  structuring of not just human beings but  also what we would view is as lower  beings less developed beings beings that  are lacking in rationality or perhaps  even in sentience and so Augustine sort  of reconstructs with his student how it  is that you know all these these levels  to human being are built upon each other  and he also takes a few little you could  say excursions in  to discussion of the relation between  some of them that are particularly  important and I do want to signal that  right off the bat before we jump into  looking at these different levels  because his framing of it works along  these lines he says that there is a law  in eternal law by which it is just that  everything should have its due order and  he says you know this applies to  governments and temporal laws and  Nations and then he says well what about  human beings does this apply to us and  then he says tell me are you absolutely  certain that you're alive to a student  and the student says yeah of course I am  there's nothing more certain that I know  of and then he says can you distinguish  between living and knowing that you live  can you make this distinction there's  the fact that you're alive and the  processes of living and then there's you  knowing that you're alive and it doesn't  have to be some sort of great you know  systemic worked out knowledge it can  just be I know that I'm alive I'm self  conscious of the fact that I not only am  conscious but that I live and so he goes  on and he he talks about the difference  between us and animals but then he gets  to another point a little bit later on  so he says animals live and as we now  see they live without reason and he says  so now you see that you know what you  said you did not know not everything  which lives knows that it's lit that it  lives though everything which knows that  it lived necessarily lives and so the  student says yeah that's right so then  he asked well which of these is better  which of these is more excellent and the  student says to know that we live now  here's where it gets really interesting  Augustine then says do you think that to  know that we live is better than life  itself  and you might say whoa that's you know  how are you going to do that if you're  not alive right knowing that you're  alive is basically predicated on the  assumption that you are living he's not  saying oh well let's strip away life  from you and  compare them side-by-side because  there's something else going on here he  says do you perhaps understand that  knowledge is a higher and purer form of  life since no one can know unless he has  understanding what else is understanding  and here's a beautiful phrase here then  a life brighter and more perfect through  the very life of the mind so this is a  really important little passage here  because what it clarifies for us is that  as we move from these lower levels that  we share in common with animals and  plants up into these these higher levels  we're not talking about functions that  are totally separated off as if there's  these you know hermetically sealed off  containers or something like that  instead you have and this is a very  common notion in ancient philosophy you  find it among the Platonic and then  later Neoplatonic and also among the  stoic philosophers there are these  degrees of being and as you move up the  degrees of being these are differences  in an intensity that sort of build off  of each other so that's an important  consideration another important  consideration that he brings up too and  he's talking about knowledge and he says  you fully grasped and explained my own  view provided that knowledge can never  be evil and then Augustine says yes I  think knowledge cannot be evil unless we  give the word a new meaning and use  knowledge for practical experience  practical experience you know we say we  have knowledge but that that actually  can be bad in some respects he gives an  example of being punished but we might  also think it's about the practical  experiences about doing things that  we're not supposed to do and getting  away with them right how to do that so  then there's this long discussion about  what it is that we human beings have in  common with other things and what we  don't so  he says whatever it is by which human  beings are superior to beasts whether  mind or spirit or whether either of them  as the correct term because we find both  in Sacred Scripture  if this governs and controls all the  other elements of which the human being  is composed then the human being is duly  ordered so when the top part mind or  spirit or reason is running the show  then we have proper order within our  souls and we are following the eternal  law which is guiding us towards towards  that right so how could we how could we  go wrong so he says think about what it  is that we have in common with other  things plants animals humans all share  in nourishment they consume something  right plants don't go around and you  know shovel food or anything like that  there are a few carnivorous plants of  course you know like the Venus flytrap  but for the most part they're consuming  things in a much more sedate manner  growth that's something central to  plants and to animals generation that is  reproduction if you're suffering from  you know allergies it could be because  there's so much pollen in the air and  pollen is basically one way in which  plants are gonna reproduce bite down on  you know fruit and you've got seeds in  your mouth and you're like I don't like  these well that's that's how the plant  reproduces itself and then the function  of Health a sort of you know overall  organic whatever we want to call it good  state of the creature those that we  share in common then at the level of  animals we have sense perception and  he's talking about you know what it is  that we might be better or worse than  but we all share in some sort of sense  perception  he says beasts have sight hearing smell  taste touch off and more keenly than we  do we also have movement he talks about  strength vigor muscular power Swift and  easy movement of the body will just  consolidate all that into movement and  the capacity for it and then seeking  bodily pleasure and avoiding bodily pain  says we have this all in common with  beasts with the non-rational animals now  some thinkers in the past would say what  makes us good is what we don't have in  common with the the animals the higher  part of our self Augustine actually  subdivides that he says not all of how  we differ from the animals is  necessarily good by itself it has to be  again in proper order so what does he  bring up very interestingly he says that  there are some other things that we have  that beasts don't seem to share but  which are not the highest qualities of a  human being  laughing and joking our sense of humor  you know that's fun but it's not the  highest part of ourselves  he also says there's love of praise and  glory and ambition although the beasts  don't have these passions we must  suppose that we're better than the  beasts because we have them why because  when this creating is not subject to  reason it actually makes us miserable or  wretched so you know we're different  than the animals in that for example I  mean you could say animals have social  hierarchies but animals don't pride  themselves on how much money they have  in the bank or other you know quite  arbitrary signs of their social  hierarchies it tends to be based on  things a little bit closer to their  bodily nature than the you know crazy  stuff that looking at it from a  perspective of reason the crazy stuff  that we think actually matters so much  so what what is the upshot here well if  we're going to be properly ordered that  is in accordance with the eternal law  the highest should rule the the lower  not the reverse so we shouldn't simply  be driven by our desire for bodily  pleasure or avoiding bodily pain if we  make that the central priority and we  only use our reason or mind or knowledge  to try to do that we're actually not  just going to end up with  wrong consequences in actions we're  screwing ourselves up we're subverting  the proper ordering of things  likewise if our whole life is spent just  about you know laughing and joking and  you might say oh what about those poor  comedians well you know it's possible to  be a comedy writer and not to make  comedy the only thing in your life that  you're always looking for a laugh you  